# Thinkway Engineering & Deployment Policy

## 1. Database policy

| Environment | Project ref | Role |
|-------------|-------------|------|
| **Development** | `hsxrewjcbvmbkqdlzjhs` | Default for all development, debugging, testing, and schema changes |
| **Production** | `ienowhwfyxoqtzbgltno` | Never modify unless the user **explicitly** requests a production deployment, migration, hotfix, or operational change |

**Before any database write**, state which project will be modified.

If the target is Production and the user has not explicitly approved it, **stop and ask for confirmation**.

## 2. Permanent release workflow

**Development is always updated first. Production is only updated after explicit user approval.**

See `docs/RELEASE_WORKFLOW.md`.

| Surface | URL | Supabase | Deploy |
|---|---|---|---|
| Development | `https://dev.thinkwaymedia.com` | `hsxrewjcbvmbkqdlzjhs` | Auto from branch `develop` |
| Production | `https://app.thinkwaymedia.com` | `ienowhwfyxoqtzbgltno` | Only after explicit approval |

The in-app environment switch **navigates between deployments**. Never switch Supabase/Redis inside one running app.

### Phase 1 – Development

1. Implement and test locally against Development Supabase (`hsxrewjcbvmbkqdlzjhs`)
2. Commit and push to `develop` (auto-deploys Development)
3. Verify: TypeScript, Build, Operations Center, Redis, BullMQ, Discovery Worker, Release Readiness
4. Do **not** modify Production Supabase in this phase

### Phase 2 – Production (approval required)

Before any Production action, provide a deployment summary (files changed, migrations, schema impact, data impact, downtime). Wait for approval before:

- Deploying Production (`vercel deploy --prod` or commit marker `[deploy-production]`)
- Running migrations on Production
- Writing to the Production database (`ienowhwfyxoqtzbgltno`)

**Production is never the first place where a feature is tested.**

## 3. Migration policy

- All future database changes must be delivered as **migrations**.
- Never make manual schema changes directly in Production.
- Every migration must first be validated against the Development database.

## 4. Production safety

Before any destructive action, explicitly confirm:

- Target project  
- Target environment  
- Expected impact  

Examples: `DROP`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`, `ALTER TABLE`, database restore, bulk updates, schema changes.

If Production is the target, **wait for user approval** before executing.

## 5. Operations Center

The Operations Center (`/operations`) is the **single source of truth** for deployment health. It must clearly display:

- Environment (Development / Preview / Production)  
- Connected Supabase project reference  
- Expected project reference  
- Verification status (aligned / mismatch)  
- Build information, git commit, deployment timestamp, deployment ID  
- Overall health breakdown  
- Explainable warnings + suggested actions  

A production deployment must **immediately** reveal if it is connected to the wrong Supabase project.

## 6. Release policy

Every production release should include:

- Build verification  
- TypeScript validation  
- Lint validation  
- Database migration status (if applicable)  
- Smoke test  
- Operations Center verification  
- Release summary  

## 7. Communication policy

Before executing any action that affects infrastructure, databases, or production services, clearly state:

- What will change  
- Which environment will be affected  
- Whether the action is reversible  
- Any risks or prerequisites  

**Never assume production approval. Always ask if there is any uncertainty.**
